[NativeAOT] Pad code sections with a NOP on ARM64, LoongArch64 and RISC-V

ILC fills the alignment gaps between code nodes with one repeated byte: 0x90
on x86 and x64, zero everywhere else. The field holding it is a single byte,
which is why only x86 and x64 ever got a NOP - on a fixed-width target a NOP
does not fit in a byte. Every other architecture therefore pads its
executable sections with a word that is not an instruction: 0x00000000 is
udf #0 on ARM64 and an invalid encoding on RISC-V and LoongArch64. Anything
that decodes the section linearly - a disassembler, a debugger, a loader
that validates the code it is handed - reads garbage between methods.

Make the padding a pattern instead of a byte, keep it in phase with the
section offset, and give the fixed-width targets their NOP:

    ARM64          nop                  d503201f
    LoongArch64    andi $r0, $r0, 0     03400000
    RISC-V         c.nop                0001

RISC-V uses the compressed NOP rather than addi x0, x0, 0 because the JIT
emits RVC, so a method can end two bytes short of a four-byte boundary. A
four-byte pattern would put 0x0000 in that gap, which is the illegal
encoding - exactly the hole this change is meant to close.

x86 and x64 are unaffected, and the output does not grow: the gaps were
already there, they just held zeros.
